# hlsdemux leaves the timestamps of a fragmented MP4 (ISOBMFF) stream where the media puts them.
# qtdemux times each fragment from its tfdt box rather than from the playlist position hlsdemux
# stamps on the fragment's first buffer (qtdemux_parse_moof drops that fragment_start once a tfdt
# is present), while the stream's segment starts at the playlist's stream time. Media whose first
# decode time is not 0 -- Apple's packager starts at 10 s -- therefore plays that much late:
# nothing renders until the clock reaches the first sample, the position runs on from 0 all the
# same, and playback ends that far past the duration.
#
# hlsdemux2 maps the two. It reads the earliest decode time of the variant stream's first movie
# fragment, takes its difference from that fragment's stream time as the stream's presentation
# offset, and gives an ISOBMFF rendition the variant stream's offset so the two stay in sync;
# the base class shifts each stream's segment by its offset. The legacy base class already
# applies a subclass's offset (get_presentation_offset, which dashdemux implements), so this
# carries hlsdemux2's reading and policy over. A stream's data is held from its first byte until
# its offset is known, so its first segment carries it: the variant stream's until its first
# movie fragment, a rendition's until the variant stream has one, across the rendition's own
# fragment boundaries since its fragments can finish first. The variant stream's first movie
# fragment also maps internal time to stream time for the subtitle renditions, as the first PCR
# of an MPEG-TS fragment does.
#
# Applied by build_deps with patch -p1.
